---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/paper.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

// Every number here is traceable to the paper's main text and checked by
// `node scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs --slug alphamissense`.
// Note on what is deliberately absent: the released data files classify variants with numeric
// score cutoffs, but the paper states only that the cutoff is calibrated to 90% precision on
// ClinVar. The specific boundaries could not be sourced to anything citable, so they are not
// asserted here -- an earlier version of this page gave them as 0.340 and 0.564.
const title = "Accurate Proteome-Wide Missense Variant Effect Prediction with AlphaMissense";
const description = "Technical summary of AlphaMissense (Cheng et al., Google DeepMind, Science 2023): fine-tuning AlphaFold on human and primate population frequency data to classify all 71 million possible human missense variants.";
const readingTime = "22 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article" noindex={true}>
  <article class="paper-article">
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/papers/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Paper Summaries
      </a>
    </nav>

    <header class="paper-header">
      <div class="paper-meta-bar">
        <span class="paper-tag">Protein Structure & Clinical Variant Prediction</span>
        <span class="paper-venue-badge">Science · 19 September 2023 (Research Article)</span>
        <span class="paper-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="paper-title">{title}</h1>

      <div class="paper-authors-box">
        <div><strong>Authors:</strong> Jun Cheng*, Guido Novati, Joshua Pan, Clare Bycroft, Akvilė Žemgulytė, Taylor Applebaum, Alexander Pritzel, Lai Hong Wong, Michal Zielinski, Tobias Sargeant, Rosalia G. Schneider, Andrew W. Senior, John Jumper, Demis Hassabis, Pushmeet Kohli*, Žiga Avsec* (16 authors)</div>
        <div><strong>Affiliation:</strong> Google DeepMind, London, UK</div>
        <div><strong>DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adg7492" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--color-accent);">10.1126/science.adg7492</a> · Science 381, eadg7492</div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <p class="paper-lead">
      Genome sequencing finds far more missense variants than anyone can interpret: more than
      <strong>4 million</strong> have been observed in humans, and only a small fraction carry a clinical
      annotation. <strong>AlphaMissense</strong> attacks the gap by fine-tuning AlphaFold on
      <strong>human and primate variant population frequency data</strong> — weak labels rather than
      human-curated ones — and predicting a pathogenicity probability for every possible substitution. It
      classifies <strong>89% of all missense variants</strong> as likely benign or likely pathogenic
      (<strong>32% pathogenic, 57% benign</strong>) at a cutoff calibrated to <strong>90% precision on
      ClinVar</strong>, reaching an <strong>auROC of 0.940 on 18,924 held-out ClinVar variants</strong>
      against 0.911 for EVE.
    </p>

    <div class="paper-toc">
      <div class="paper-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="paper-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#summary">1. The Variant Interpretation Gap</a></li>
        <li><a href="#architecture">2. Architecture: AlphaFold as a Foundation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#weak-labels">3. The Central Trick: Weak Labels from Population Frequency</a></li>
        <li><a href="#benchmarks">4. Clinical and Experimental Benchmarks</a></li>
        <li><a href="#proteome">5. Saturating the Proteome</a></li>
        <li><a href="#emergent">6. Emergent Properties: Essentiality, Structure, Disorder</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mathematical-formulation">7. Mathematical Formulation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#failure-modes">8. Limitations & What the Score Does Not Mean</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <section id="summary" class="paper-section">
      <h2>1. The Variant Interpretation Gap</h2>
      <p>
        A missense variant changes one amino acid. Most are harmless; a few cause disease; and for the
        overwhelming majority nobody knows, which is why "variant of uncertain significance" is the commonest
        result a clinical sequencing lab returns. Of the more than <strong>4 million observed missense
        variants</strong>, only a small share have been curated into a clinical database.
      </p>
      <p>
        The paper's framing is that this is a labelling problem masquerading as a modelling problem. Curated
        labels are scarce, biased toward well-studied genes, and shaped by the very heuristics a model would
        be asked to learn. AlphaMissense's answer is to avoid them during training.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="architecture" class="paper-section">
      <h2>2. Architecture: AlphaFold as a Foundation</h2>

      <h3>What is inherited, and what changes</h3>
      <p>
        The implementation <strong>closely follows AlphaFold, with minor architectural changes</strong>. The
        inputs are the reference protein sequence together with the multiple sequence alignment that AlphaFold
        already relies on; the structural understanding is not re-learned but reused.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pathogenicity readout comes from a <strong>masked residue prediction head</strong>: the score is
        the <strong>difference in predicted log-likelihood between the alternate and the reference
        residue</strong> at that position. This is the standard variant-effect construction from protein
        language models, applied to a network whose representation was built for structure.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="target" size={15} /> Why start from a structure model at all
        </div>
        <p>
          A protein language model learns which residues are tolerated from sequence alignments. AlphaFold
          learns that <em>plus</em> the geometry those alignments imply. The bet is that a representation
          which can place a residue in three dimensions already encodes why substituting it matters — and
          the emergent results in section 6, none of which were trained for, are the evidence offered.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="weak-labels" class="paper-section">
      <h2>3. The Central Trick: Weak Labels from Population Frequency</h2>
      <p>
        Rather than fine-tuning on ClinVar, AlphaMissense fine-tunes on <strong>human and primate variant
        population frequency data</strong>, described in the paper as <strong>weak labels</strong>, explicitly
        <strong>avoiding bias from human-curated annotations</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The logic is population-genetic rather than clinical. A substitution observed at appreciable frequency
        in humans or in closely related primates has survived purifying selection and is therefore probably
        tolerated; one that never appears may not be. Neither signal requires a clinician to have written
        anything down, and critically, neither is contaminated by the annotation practices that a
        ClinVar-trained model would otherwise learn to reproduce.
      </p>
      <p>
        The confidence calibration is then done separately, on known disease variants — so curated data enters
        the pipeline only to set a threshold, never to shape the representation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="benchmarks" class="paper-section">
      <h2>4. Clinical and Experimental Benchmarks</h2>

      <h3>ClinVar, held out</h3>
      <p>
        On <strong>18,924 held-out ClinVar test variants</strong>, AlphaMissense achieves an
        <strong>auROC of 0.940</strong>, against <strong>0.911</strong> for the Evolutionary model of Variant
        Effect (EVE), a difference the paper reports at <strong>P = 0.001</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3>De novo variants in developmental disorders</h3>
      <p>
        On the <strong>Deciphering Developmental Disorders (DDD)</strong> benchmark, AlphaMissense achieves an
        <strong>auROC of 0.809</strong>. This is the harder and more honest test: DDD variants are <em>de
        novo</em>, so they cannot have been seen in the population-frequency data the model was fine-tuned on.
      </p>

      <h3>Multiplexed assays of variant effect</h3>
      <p>
        The third axis is experimental rather than clinical — <strong>ProteinGym</strong>, a collection of
        multiplexed assays of variant effect (deep mutational scans). Performance here is measured by rank
        correlation with the assay, and it matters because it tests the score against a physical measurement
        with no curation in the loop at all.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Benchmark</th>
              <th>What it tests</th>
              <th>Reported result</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>ClinVar (held out)</strong></td>
              <td>Agreement with expert clinical classification</td>
              <td><strong>auROC 0.940</strong> on 18,924 variants (EVE: 0.911, P = 0.001)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>DDD de novo</strong></td>
              <td>Variants absent from population data by construction</td>
              <td><strong>auROC 0.809</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>ProteinGym (MAVE/DMS)</strong></td>
              <td>Rank agreement with direct experimental measurement</td>
              <td>Evaluated by Spearman correlation across assays</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="proteome" class="paper-section">
      <h2>5. Saturating the Proteome</h2>

      <h3>Two counts that are not the same number</h3>
      <p>
        AlphaMissense predicts the pathogenicity of all <strong>216 million possible single amino acid
        changes</strong> across <strong>19,233 canonical human proteins</strong>. That is the space of amino
        acid substitutions. The subset reachable by a single nucleotide change — the missense variants that
        can actually occur — is <strong>71 million</strong>, and the paper describes this as
        <strong>saturating the human proteome</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="paper-callout">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> The coverage result that makes it a resource
        </div>
        <p>
          Of <strong>69.5 million variants unobserved in gnomAD</strong> — the ones with no population
          frequency to read off, and therefore the ones a frequency-based method has nothing to say about —
          AlphaMissense makes a confident prediction for <strong>61.7 million (88.8%)</strong>. That is the
          claim that turns a good classifier into a lookup table for clinical genetics: the coverage is
          concentrated exactly where the evidence was missing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Across the whole set, <strong>89%</strong> of missense variants are classified as either likely benign
        or likely pathogenic — <strong>32% likely pathogenic and 57% likely benign</strong> — using a cutoff
        chosen to yield <strong>90% precision on ClinVar</strong>. The remaining ~11% stay ambiguous, and the
        paper does not pretend otherwise.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="emergent" class="paper-section">
      <h2>6. Emergent Properties: Essentiality, Structure, Disorder</h2>
      <p>
        The results in this section were not trained for, which is what makes them interesting.
      </p>

      <h3>Gene-level essentiality</h3>
      <p>
        The <strong>average pathogenicity score of a gene predicts its cell essentiality</strong>, and does so
        well enough to identify <strong>short essential genes that existing statistical approaches are
        underpowered to detect</strong>. Population-constraint metrics need mutational opportunity to
        accumulate; a short gene does not provide it, so constraint-based methods lose power exactly where a
        per-variant model does not.
      </p>

      <h3>Structured domains versus disordered regions</h3>
      <p>
        Variants located in structured domains receive <strong>higher pathogenicity scores than variants in
        disordered regions</strong>, with disorder predicted by AlphaFold itself. The paper is candid that
        this cuts both ways: it also notes <strong>reduced performance on variants at residues predicted to
        be disordered</strong>. A model grounded in structure is least reliable where there is least
        structure.
      </p>

      <h3>Protein stability</h3>
      <p>
        The analysis also reports that <strong>disease-causing variants are more likely to reside in thermally
        stable proteins</strong> — a property of the disease-variant distribution rather than of the model,
        surfaced by having scores for everything.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="mathematical-formulation" class="paper-section">
      <h2>7. Mathematical Formulation</h2>
      <p>
        Pathogenicity is read out of the masked residue prediction head as the log-likelihood difference
        between the alternate residue <Katex tex="a" /> and the reference residue <Katex tex="r" /> at
        position <Katex tex="i" />, conditioned on the surrounding sequence <Katex tex="\mathbf{x}" /> and its
        multiple sequence alignment <Katex tex="\mathcal{M}" />:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="s(v) \;\propto\; \log p\big(a \mid \mathbf{x}_{\setminus i}, \mathcal{M}\big) - \log p\big(r \mid \mathbf{x}_{\setminus i}, \mathcal{M}\big)" />
      </div>
      <p>
        Two things follow from the form. It is a <em>difference</em>, so any per-position bias in the head
        cancels — the score asks how much worse the alternate is than the reference, not how likely either is
        in absolute terms. And it is defined for every substitution at every position without needing labels,
        which is what makes proteome-wide saturation possible at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        The final classification is a threshold on the calibrated score, chosen so that precision on ClinVar
        reaches 90%:
      </p>
      <div class="paper-equation-block">
        <Katex tex="\text{class}(v) = \begin{cases} \text{likely pathogenic} & s(v) > \tau_{\text{high}} \\ \text{ambiguous} & \tau_{\text{low}} \le s(v) \le \tau_{\text{high}} \\ \text{likely benign} & s(v) < \tau_{\text{low}} \end{cases}" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="failure-modes" class="paper-section">
      <h2>8. Limitations & What the Score Does Not Mean</h2>

      <h3>Where the model is weakest</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Disordered residues.</strong> The paper explicitly reports reduced performance there. Intrinsically disordered regions are a large fraction of the human proteome and are enriched for regulatory and signalling function.</li>
        <li><strong>Coding, missense, single substitutions only.</strong> Nothing here addresses non-coding variants, splice-disrupting synonymous changes, indels, or structural variation — which is most of the variation in a genome.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The label question the design raises</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Population frequency is a proxy for tolerance, not for benignity.</strong> A variant can be common and still contribute risk, particularly for late-onset or low-penetrance disease, where selection has had little to act on. The weak-label design buys freedom from curation bias at the cost of inheriting this one.</li>
        <li><strong>Primate frequency data imports its own assumption</strong> — that a substitution tolerated in a related species is tolerated in the human protein context.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What "likely pathogenic" licenses clinically</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A calibrated 90%-precision cutoff is a population-level guarantee, not a per-variant one.</strong> The paper offers a resource for prioritisation; ACMG-style clinical classification requires evidence types — segregation, functional assay, allele frequency in matched controls — that a sequence model does not supply.</li>
        <li><strong>89% classified is not 89% solved.</strong> The remaining ambiguous fraction is not random: it concentrates where the model has least signal, which is where the hard cases already were.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="paper-callout callout-info">
        <div class="paper-callout-title">
          <Icon name="book" size={15} /> Source note
        </div>
        <p>
          Written against the full text of the published Science article (13 pp). Figures cited as "fig. S…"
          in the paper live in the supplementary materials, which are not part of this reading — where a
          number appears only there, this page describes the finding without quoting the value. The page is
          checked by <code>scripts/verify-paper-claims.mjs</code>, which flags any number the paper's own
          text does not contain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
